# Contributing

Use GitHub Discussions for support and early ideas, and Issues for
reproducible defects or agreed work.

## Verification

```bash
cargo xtask ci        # the complete gate — everything below it, in this order
cargo xtask check     # compile everything, tests and examples included
cargo xtask lint      # fmt, taplo, typos, clippy denied, deny, machete
cargo xtask features  # every feature compiled on its own, one build each
cargo xtask test      # tests, then the examples inside the documentation
cargo xtask docs      # rustdoc, warnings denied
{% if project_kind == "published_library" -%}
cargo xtask package   # what publishing would ship, and a consumer building it
{% else -%}
cargo xtask dist      # the shippable artifacts, built for this machine
{% endif -%}
cargo xtask fuzz      # builds the fuzz targets; a run is started by hand
```

`cargo xtask ci` is the gate CI runs, so there is nothing to remember to pass
by hand. Run it before opening a pull request.

CI runs the same tasks one job each rather than one after another: they share
no results, so running them in sequence means the slowest one starts last.
That is why a red build names a task — `lint`, `features`, `test` — and the
command that reproduces it is that task, not the whole gate.

The gate shells out to six tools; without them installed it fails on the
missing command rather than on the code:

```bash
cargo install cargo-deny cargo-machete cargo-hack cargo-nextest taplo-cli typos-cli
```

`taplo` formats TOML, which nothing else formats; `typos` reads prose and
identifiers, where a misspelling compiles and so survives every other check.
Both are fast enough to sit in the gate.

Tests run under nextest, which gives each test its own process — see
`.config/nextest.toml` for why that is worth a separate runner. Doctests are
the one thing it does not run, so `cargo test --doc` runs alongside.
{% if project_kind != "published_library" %}
`dist` needs [dist](https://github.com/axodotdev/cargo-dist) and is not part
of the gate: it is there to try a packaging change locally rather than by
tagging a release and reading the CI log.
{% endif %}
`fuzz` builds every fuzz crate it finds and stops there. A fuzzer is a search
rather than a check — it answers "not yet" until it answers "here", and
neither answer is a thing to hold a change to — so neither the gate nor any
workflow ever starts one. Compiling a target is a check, though, and the one
nothing else performs: a fuzz crate is a workspace of its own, so a change to
the API a target calls would otherwise break it silently and stay broken until
somebody thought to fuzz. It finds nothing and succeeds where there is no fuzz
crate.

A real search needs a nightly toolchain, cargo-fuzz, and someone to ask for
it, from inside the crate that carries the target:

```bash
cargo install cargo-fuzz
cd <crate>/fuzz && cargo +nightly fuzz run <target>
```

No corpus is committed, and the scaffolded `.gitignore` keeps it that way. An
input that found something belongs in a regression test in the crate it broke,
where the gate runs it on every change, not in a directory the gate never
opens.

## Dependencies

`Cargo.lock` is committed and every task above passes `--locked`, so a change
that moves a version moves the lockfile in the same commit. That is what makes
a red build mean this code broke, not somebody else's release.

A new dependency brings its license with it, and `deny.toml` lists only the
licenses this project has already accepted — adding one is a line someone
writes and a reviewer reads.

## Toolchains

`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the compiler this project is developed with, and
`rustup` installs it on the first `cargo` command.
{% if project_kind == "published_library" %}
`rust-version` in `Cargo.toml` is the separate, older floor promised to
whoever depends on this crate. CI builds with exactly that toolchain, so
raising it is a deliberate change rather than a side effect of reaching for a
newer API.
{% else %}
There is no `rust-version`: nobody compiles this project but the people
working on it, and the pinned toolchain is the floor.
{% endif %}
## Releasing

release-plz keeps a pull request open carrying the next version and the
changelog entries for everything merged since the last release. Merging it is
the decision.
{% if project_kind == "published_library" %}
Merging it publishes: the `Release` workflow uploads to crates.io through
Trusted Publishing, tags the commit, and opens the GitHub release. No registry
token exists in this repository — CI proves its identity with a short-lived
token instead.

That depends on three things done once on crates.io, in this order:

1. Publish `0.1.0` by hand. Trusted Publishing cannot create a crate that does
   not exist yet, and there is no pending-publisher flow.
2. Add a Trusted Publisher to the crate: this repository, `release.yml`, and
   the `release` environment. Those three together are the identity.
3. Turn on "Trusted Publishing only", so a leaked API token cannot publish
   this crate at all.
{% else %}
Merging it only bumps the version. Shipping is a separate, deliberate act:
dispatch the `Release` workflow with the tag to cut. At its default it
dry-runs and publishes nothing, so there is no way to ship by accident. With
a real tag, the run stops at the release gate until a reviewer approves —
before a single artifact is built — then `dist` builds every target in
`dist-workspace.toml`, attaches the checksums, source tarball and bill of
materials, and creates the GitHub release.

That workflow is generated, not written by hand: `dist generate` writes
`.github/workflows/release.yml` from `dist-workspace.toml`. Re-run it after
changing that file and commit the result — `dist generate --check` reports
drift.

Not `dist init`: that command is for a project with no dist configuration
yet, and it rewrites `dist-workspace.toml` from its own template, deleting
every comment — which is where the reasons for each setting live.
{% endif %}
## As the project grows

The gate covers what every change must pass from day one. Some tools only
earn their place when the code grows the problem they answer, so they are
named here rather than installed:

- **proptest** — property-based testing: state what must hold for any input
  and let the framework hunt for a counterexample. Worth it the day a
  function's edge cases outnumber the examples anyone would write by hand.
{% if project_kind == "published_library" -%}
{% if fuzzing -%}
- **cargo-fuzz** — the harness under `fuzz/` is scaffolded and waiting; give
  its target a function that consumes untrusted bytes the day one exists.
{% else -%}
- **cargo-fuzz** — coverage-guided random input until something panics, for
  the day this crate parses bytes it did not produce.
{% endif -%}
- **trybuild** — asserts that code which must not compile does not, with the
  error it should give. For macro work and API-misuse design.
{% else -%}
- **cargo-fuzz** — coverage-guided random input until something panics. A
  fuzzer calls a function, not a running server — the HTTP layer is the
  framework's, already fuzzed upstream — so the day a crate under `crates/`
  parses outside input, run `cargo fuzz init` inside that crate and point
  the target at that function. `cargo xtask fuzz` finds it from then on and
  keeps it compiling; no wiring needed.
{% endif -%}
- **cargo-insta** — snapshot testing: assert a complex output once, review
  its diffs forever after. For output too large to write expectations by
  hand.
- **loom** — model-checks every interleaving of concurrent code using
  atomics or locks; the data race a thousand test runs never hit, found by
  exhaustion.
- **sanitizers** (`-Z sanitizer=address,thread`) — undefined-behavior
  detection where Miri cannot follow: FFI boundaries and real threads.
- **criterion** or **iai-callgrind** — harnesses for `cargo xtask bench`
  when "did this get slower?" needs a number; the second measures
  instructions rather than time, which is what makes it stable enough
  for CI.
- **cargo-vet** — recorded audits of dependency source, shared between
  projects. For code whose compromise would be a security event, not
  a bug.

## Pull requests

Keep each pull request focused, and add or update a test at the public
behavior seam being changed.

Pull requests merge by rebase: every commit lands on the default branch
exactly as written, and release-plz reads those subjects to build the
changelog and choose the next version. That is why each rule below is about
the individual commit, not the pull request:

- Conventional Commits: `<type>(scope): <description>`, imperative mood, and
  CI checks it — on every commit, and on the pull request title. The types are
  `build`, `chore`, `ci`, `docs`, `feat`, `fix`, `perf`, `refactor`, `revert`,
  `style` and `test`. Mark a breaking change with `!` before the colon — a
  break that does not say so ships as a patch.
- Every commit carries a `Signed-off-by` line (`git commit -s`) under the
  [Developer Certificate of Origin](https://developercertificate.org/),
  which CI checks commit by commit against each commit's own author.
- Hard-wrap commit body lines at 72 characters.
- Atomic commits: one self-sufficient change per commit. Each one lands on the
  default branch and becomes its own changelog line, so "and also fixed an
  unrelated thing on the way" belongs in a commit of its own.
